Software Requirements Specification (SRS)
DRUKSAFE — AI-Powered Flood Prediction & Early Warning System
Introduction
Purpose This document specifies the complete functional and non-functional requirements for
DrukSafe, an AI-powered flood prediction and early warning system developed specifically for
the Kingdom of Bhutan.
It serves as the main technical blueprint for the development team during the TechBiz
Bootcamp and as a reference document for future deployment with the Government of Bhutan
and NCHM.

Project Overview
DrukSafe is a web-based dashboard that:
• Monitors real-time rainfall data across Bhutan’s highest flood-risk dzongkhags
• Calculates flood risk scores using AI-assisted logic
• Automatically sends SMS alerts to farmers, local leaders, and disaster management
officials when risk thresholds are crossed
Unlike current reactive systems, DrukSafe is predictive where it aims to warn communities
several hours before floodwaters arrive.

Scope In Scope (v1.0):
• Real-time rainfall monitoring via Open-Meteo API
• River level estimation from rainfall accumulation
• District-level flood risk scoring (0–100)
• Interactive map dashboard with colour-coded risk zones
• Automated SMS alerts (English + Dzongkha)
• Simulation/demo mode for presentations and training
Intended Audience
• Project supervisors and evaluators (TechBiz Bootcamp)
• Development team
• Government stakeholders (NCHM, DDM)
• Future maintainers and funders
Overall Description
Product Perspective DrukSafe is a standalone web application designed to fill a critical gap in
Bhutan’s current Early Warning Systems by shifting from “detect-and-warn” to “predict-andprotect”.

User Classes
• General Users / Officials: View dashboard and risk status
• Administrators: Manage alert recipients, thresholds, and simulation mode
• End Recipients: Farmers and local leaders who receive SMS alerts
Operating Environment
• Modern web browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge)
• Desktop and large presentation screens
• Internet connection required for live data (simulation mode works offline)
Functional Requirements

3.1 Dashboard & Map
• The system shall display a Bhutan district map using Leaflet.js with GeoJSON overlay.
• The map shall colour-code the four target districts in real time based on current risk
score.
• Clicking a district shall open a detail panel showing rainfall, river level, risk score, and
timestamp.
• Dashboard shall show a global status indicator (ALL CLEAR / ADVISORY / ALERT ACTIVE).
• Last Updated timestamp shall refresh every 15 minutes.
3.2 Data Collection & Processing
• The system shall fetch rainfall forecast data from Open-Meteo API for the 4 target
districts every 15 minutes.
• The system shall estimate river level rise (%) based on upstream rainfall accumulation.
• Data shall be stored locally in SQLite with timestamps.
• If API is unavailable, the system shall show last known data with a “Data Stale” warning.

3.3 AI Risk Scoring Engine
• The risk engine shall calculate a flood risk score (0–100) for each district every 15
minutes.
• Score calculation shall consider rainfall forecast, estimated river rise, and district
sensitivity.
• Primary engine: Rules-based logic (e.g., rainfall >80mm AND river rise >20% = High Risk).
• Optional upgrade: Machine learning model (scikit-learn) trained on historical data.

3.4 Alert System
• The system shall automatically send SMS alerts when risk score > 70 (High).
• Alerts shall be sent in both English and Dzongkha.
• Alert message shall include district name, risk level, key readings, and recommended
action.
• Twilio API shall be used for real SMS (with on-screen simulation fallback).
• The system shall avoid sending duplicate alerts within 1 hour.

3.5 Simulation Mode
• The dashboard shall have a “Simulate Monsoon Event” feature.
• In simulation mode, rainfall and river levels shall increase gradually.
• The map shall animate colour changes and trigger sample SMS alerts.
• Manual sliders for rainfall and river level shall be available.
• Simulation mode shall be clearly labelled to avoid confusion with live data.

Non-Functional Requirements
Performance
• Dashboard shall load within 5 seconds.
• Risk score calculation shall complete within 3 seconds.
• SMS dispatch shall occur within 10 seconds of threshold breach.

Reliability
• Target 95% uptime during monsoon season.
• System shall fallback to last valid data if API fails.
• Alert sending shall retry up to 3 times.
Usability
• The system shall be usable by non-technical government officers after a short briefing.
• All text shall be available in English and Dzongkha.
• Colour coding shall follow WCAG AA accessibility standards.

Security
• Phone numbers shall be stored encrypted.
• Twilio keys shall not be exposed in frontend code.
• Admin panel shall require password authentication.

Scalability
• Architecture shall support easy expansion to all 20 dzongkhags.
• Shall allow future integration with NCHM river gauge sensors and satellite data.
External Interfaces

APIs and Services
• Open-Meteo API: Free rainfall forecast (no key required)
• Twilio SMS API: For sending real alerts
• Leaflet.js + Bhutan GeoJSON: Interactive map
• Chart.js: Rainfall and river level charts

Hardware / Deployment
• Prototype: Vercel / GitHub Pages or small cloud instance
• Production: Small VPS during monsoon season
Constraints & Assumptions

Constraints
• Limited to 72-hour hackathon development time.
• No real-time river gauge data from NCHM during prototype.
• Twilio free trial has SMS limits.
• Open-Meteo provides forecast, not real river levels.

Assumptions
• Target areas have reliable mobile coverage for SMS.
• Recipients will act upon receiving alerts.
• Open-Meteo API remains available.
• Government will provide historical flood data for future ML training.
Business Model & Competitive Advantage

Competitive Advantage
DrukSafe is the only system that offers predictive capability + Dzongkha alerts + Bhutanspecific focus.
Revenue Streams
1. Government Licensing – Annual SaaS contract with DDM/NCHM
2. Insurance Partnership – Sell risk data to insurance companies
3. International Grants – UNDP, World Bank, ADB climate funds

Growth Roadmap
• Prototype: Hackathon (4 districts + simulation)
• Pilot: Year 1 (Government pilot + real integration)
• Scale: Year 2 (All 20 dzongkhags)
• Regional: Year 3 (Nepal, Bangladesh)

Team Roles & Responsibilities
• Frontend Developer: Map, charts, UI, simulation
• Backend / Data Developer: API integration, risk engine, SMS
• AI / Data Analyst: Risk scoring logic and ML model
• Business / Pitch Lead: Research, impact stories, pitch delivery
